schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Self-Custody Crypto Wallets
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    An app that lets a person store, send, and receive digital assets
    directly, controlled by a public address (shareable, like an account
    number) and a secret seed phrase (12 or 24 words granting full access),
    without a bank as intermediary.
  source_traditions:
    - Bitcoin/Ethereum-era self-custody wallet design (public/private key pairs)
    - browser-extension and mobile wallet UX patterns (MetaMask, Coinbase Wallet, Trust Wallet, Rainbow)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used as the entry point for a beginner-facing program: contrasts
    "bank account you don't control" with "wallet you fully control,"
    then routes beginners to a specific first wallet based on their goal.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - custodial wallets (e.g., exchange-held funds) as the implicit contrast case, though not directly named in this article
    - hardware/cold wallets as a more security-hardened but higher-friction alternative (referenced only via the "related" article link, not explained here)
  important_distinctions:
    - public address (safe to share) vs secret/seed phrase (never share, full access)
    - self-custody (you hold the keys) vs custodial (a company holds them for you)
  tensions:
    - beginner-friendliness vs the real, unforgiving risk of an irrecoverable loss if the seed phrase is lost or leaked
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    The guide's ranked "easiest for beginners" framing risks understating
    that all four wallets carry the same fundamental self-custody risk
    (irreversible loss on seed-phrase compromise) — ease-of-setup is not the
    same axis as security posture, and the article does not clearly
    distinguish the two.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/wallet-comparison-guide.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  source_lineage: wallet-comparison-guide.md
